From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JalenLin <jalen.lin@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:04:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/22] media: coda: add the Realtek RTD129x CODA980 (VE1)

VE1 on the RTD1295/1296 is a Chips&Media CODA980 (product code 0x9800)
behind a Realtek wrapper. It runs as a CODA960 -- same CODA9 register map and
command set -- with these differences, found against Realtek's BSP driver and
the Android libvpu (both in BPI's trees):

- Power: the SRAM power domain, reset and isolation are CRT registers, and
  the wrapper must enable its command interface (CTI) before any core
  register answers; until then reads end in an SB2 timeout (0xdeadbeef).
  The reset controller (snps,dw-low-reset) has no .reset, so the reset in
  coda_hw_reset() -- run after every SEQ_END on a CODA960 -- returned early
  and left the GDI bus stopped: every later SEQ_INIT hung.
- FRAME_MEM_CTRL: bits 12:9 are the frame map type and BWB moved to bit 15.
  The CODA960 BWB bit selected a tiled field map, and every pixel was read
  from the frame's first address.
- GDI 2.0: the GDI 1.0 tables have other registers at those offsets; leave
  the GDI alone for linear frames, as libvpu does. No tiled maps.
- The firmware reads command parameters the CODA960 path never writes
  (the SPS came out as High profile with a bogus bit depth); zero them
  first. The SPS frame-crop flag is bit 2, not 3.
- No subsampled frames, a 470 KiB temp buffer, no JPEG engine, no IRAM.
- The source's chroma layout is a per-picture flag (ENC_PIC_ROT_MODE bit
  18): without it NV12 is read as planar and Cr comes from elsewhere in the
  picture.
- The CODA960 frame cache settings make it read stale reference data, so
  colour edges drift with every P-frame; leave the cache alone, as libvpu
  does.
- Early in boot the first power-on of its SRAM domain is not acked; power
  it off and on again.
- The firmware reports product 0xe428 (CODA980), version 3.0.1.

Also create V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_FORCE_KEY_FRAME, which coda_s_ctrl()
handles but nothing registered.

Only the H.264 encoder is exposed: it is what was verified (1080p at about
60 fps, from the HDMI receiver through DMA-BUF).

Also: take a memory-region if the device tree gives one. Frame buffers
are allocated when a stream starts, and on a board whose SD card is busy
CMA can fail to move page cache out of the way in time (-ENOMEM with
three quarters of the area free); a pool of its own never has to.

Source: https://github.com/JalenLin/BPI-W2-linux

Signed-off-by: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
